<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euripides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 480–<a href="/years/406-bce">406 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euripides was an Athenian tragic playwright, active from 455 BCE until his death in about 406 BCE. Eighteen or nineteen of his plays survive, more than of any other Greek tragedian, including Medea, Hippolytus, The Trojan Women and The Bacchae. His plays questioned traditional ideas about gods and heroes and gave unusual prominence to women. He spent his last years at the court of King Archelaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euripides.jpg" alt="Euripides" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NikonZ7II · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euripide_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athenian festivals produced tragedy and comedy: the surviving plays of Aeschylus, Sophocles, <b>Euripides</b> and Aristophanes remain the foundation of Western drama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Archelaus I was crowned at Olympia and Delphi for chariot racing and received the painter Zeuxis, the architect Callimachus, the poets Choerilus, Timotheus and Agathon, and <b>Euripides</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The odes of Pindar honoured victors at the sacred games, and the tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles and <b>Euripides</b>, performed at the festivals of Dionysus, explored the relations between gods and mortals; Euripides’ Bacchae dramatises the arrival of Dionysus and the punishment of those who refuse him.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The 5th and 4th centuries BCE, centred on Athens, produced the tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles and <b>Euripides</b> and the comedies of Aristophanes; history, invented as a form of inquiry by Herodotus and refined by Thucydides; the speeches of Demosthenes; and philosophy, from the dialogues of Plato to the…</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In the century after Cleisthenes its citizens beat the Persians at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE, helped to defeat them at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, built the Parthenon and staged the dramas of Aeschylus, Sophocles and <b>Euripides</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-island">Salamis Island</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Pella, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.94,23.49&amp;year=-432" aria-label="Salamis Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5135 800 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Euripides"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5585 1070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5585 1070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.94,23.49&amp;year=-432">What was here in 432 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And then, forsooth, 'tis they that face the call Of war, while we sit sheltered, hid from all Peril!—False mocking! Sooner would I stand Three times to face their battles, shield in hand, Than bear one child.&#8221;</p><footer>Medea &#183; tr. Gilbert Murray, 1906 &#183; spoken by Medea &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35451/pg35451-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What else is Wisdom? What of man's endeavour Or God's high grace, so lovely and so great? To stand from fear set free, to breathe and wait; To hold a hand uplifted over Hate; And shall not Loveliness be loved for ever?&#8221;</p><footer>The Bacchae &#183; tr. Gilbert Murray, 1902 &#183; sung by the chorus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35173/pg35173-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;How are ye blind, Ye treaders down of cities, ye that cast Temples to desolation, and lay waste Tombs, the untrodden sanctuaries where lie The ancient dead; yourselves so soon to die!&#8221;</p><footer>The Trojan Women &#183; tr. Gilbert Murray, 1905 &#183; spoken by the god Poseidon &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35171/pg35171-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
